#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228949 :question/id #uuid "6b62d830-be21-5734-8a12-115b6a5136bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21880" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank initially holds $800\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\textrm{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{800}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q = 0.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $Q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nQ_c(t) = Ae^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nQ_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $Q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + \\dfrac{1}{20}\\alpha = 0.4\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = 8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t) = Q_c(t) + Q_p(t) = Ae^{-t/20} + 8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 7,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+8 & =7 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 8 - e^{-t/20}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1403309b-bb10-5ce3-bd8f-97915bb837ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569 :topic/math-academy-id 877 :topic/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048608 :topic/math-academy-id 1713 :topic/title "Modeling Mixture Problems With First-Order Separable ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/math-academy-id 6678 :topic/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99808cf8-bbc9-54ff-a1ba-83f2a5fdeb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t) = 22.5 - 16.5e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228562} {:answer/id #uuid "a5521b71-2ca3-5f43-b4af-fa177f129bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t) = 22.5 - 16.5e^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228563} {:answer/id #uuid "ed98ee46-bedb-5d89-9d89-4c2aa24d8721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t) = 22.5 - 16.5e^{t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228564} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce4aff0-803e-599f-a905-bde82047bc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t) = 6 - 22.5e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228565} {:answer/id #uuid "3e116b4e-4acb-509c-b36b-4565f709f143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t) = 22.5 + 16.5e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3214b49d-a843-5dfc-aaf4-826626ec3984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228561}] :question/id #uuid "872cac41-3a52-503a-bac8-64cc582ee5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339385" :question/problem "A wastewater basin initially holds $750L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved pollutant.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved pollutant is pumped into the basin at a rate of $30L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. Which expression gives the amount of pollutant $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the basin $t$ minutes after pumping begins?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e1475d2-7658-5bfe-ac43-ffd1505c10be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.83kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228568} {:answer/id #uuid "f373892c-d44d-5ca4-87c6-9686668b6bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.02kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228569} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a3f476-7ca4-5e94-a291-5155c9a0a5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.11kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228570} {:answer/id #uuid "190a0242-051d-533b-8e18-6d0b767d11ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.45kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228571} {:answer/id #uuid "53ddf027-12e1-5680-907f-e7217bcbea8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.76kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228572}] :answer-field/id #uuid "60eabee9-a505-5182-8327-f4aaf6a75e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228567}] :question/id #uuid "0b497833-a441-5b14-bcbb-b0d828ec78fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339392" :question/problem "A fish hatchery tank initially holds $900L$ of water containing $10kg$ of dissolved salt.\nA feed solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of the salt is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $6$ minutes after pumping begins."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64b2a1d5-c1ff-542b-b36c-6db0efde0080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377244} {:answer/id #uuid "1e022231-3289-56f5-b77a-6fa5e2768d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.96\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377245} {:answer/id #uuid "339b8286-0b9a-5bce-8b4a-139dcdcd79c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.49\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377246} {:answer/id #uuid "3a37a27b-7d8d-5e83-886d-a22fd90988bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.02\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377247} {:answer/id #uuid "974b466b-a863-5b62-b0b7-196c75665716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.83\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339b8286-0b9a-5bce-8b4a-139dcdcd79c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.49\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377246} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e36df41-d08a-5164-b00e-7830a62b1c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377243}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ea5d5e7-af5c-553a-9742-7d54f2384984" :question/math-academy-id "q-336734" :question/problem "A nutrient-mixing tank initially holds $720\\,\\text{L}$ of solution containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of supplement in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which supplements enter the tank minus the rate at which supplements leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which supplements enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *supplement* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which supplements exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $720\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of supplement in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *supplement* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+21.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+21.6 & =3 \\\\ A & =-18.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-5/60}≈4.49\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6b3af3-595e-5f2a-9b02-bcc7246f44a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-5{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377258} {:answer/id #uuid "13d461e2-b966-5ab9-b642-de837c2fca9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8-{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377259} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d6e4d-2317-5060-be03-4689c9b28f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377260} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed3fa03-0898-5985-81d9-f0e37cb5e0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-9+16{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377261} {:answer/id #uuid "570d8df2-e03e-5a30-8e43-d41eb6518c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-2{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d6e4d-2317-5060-be03-4689c9b28f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377260} :answer-field/id #uuid "a573fd39-4b38-5a00-b6f4-d30c1ee02cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55a40904-7261-506e-bb45-5a9e0fad5627" :question/math-academy-id "q-336750" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved anti-corrosion additive.\nA solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additives enter the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $500\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+10.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+10 & =7 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d2fe13-097f-5e6f-9c33-2c32b0e27d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-7{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377265} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377266} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd3ac0f-6dcc-58e2-95b1-2de9c477d9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-1+4{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377267} {:answer/id #uuid "864fcaca-7914-585c-ab8c-d6bf235cc102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=4-{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377268} {:answer/id #uuid "c03d0083-ace6-59b4-99bc-ac215dea614c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-9+12{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377266} :answer-field/id #uuid "9149b0d8-97dc-543b-a8ef-8e274f2d81bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e940f8bd-b1c9-5052-8127-7c721b39ac69" :question/math-academy-id "q-336753" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =3 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f7f8d63-3c73-54e5-a321-064e59a6e495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377281} {:answer/id #uuid "936ab165-4ff7-5e7c-b165-a792940b2983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377282} {:answer/id #uuid "933d636b-7f9a-5a8d-877e-255718054cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=2-9{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377283} {:answer/id #uuid "e658c55e-dd94-5640-bd43-2b24b743ccb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377284} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f664fa-8d20-5d06-954d-374ce7cf36e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9+7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e658c55e-dd94-5640-bd43-2b24b743ccb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377284} :answer-field/id #uuid "66547b5b-bb7a-5943-a4eb-d13453f82fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377280}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36b5068a-10fd-5970-abe3-44c13a56330c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339382" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $450\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $450\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{15}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{15}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{15}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/15},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{15}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=9.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/15}+9.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/15}+9 & =2 \\\\ A & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495231bb-9e61-56b4-8093-d154d6a40880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.32\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377288} {:answer/id #uuid "4dec0265-b5b6-5491-9040-ac9785af058c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377289} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ed57a2-b88d-543a-a90d-9bbd77f9672a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.98\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377290} {:answer/id #uuid "53545ea8-a0a8-5fac-8720-960a278e9b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.54\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377291} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd1d53-820b-5967-911f-26f348052b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd1d53-820b-5967-911f-26f348052b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377292} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e0f293-e28a-5788-b1b2-c59142094521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377287}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f9ca797-1282-5c7a-958a-6e7a8b023c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339390" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $350\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the tank $4$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additives enter the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $350\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=7.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+7 & =8 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=7+{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of sugar in the tank $4$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(4)=7+{e}^{-4/14}≈7.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model: Inflow Equals Outflow"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229182 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228930 :question/id #uuid "2f5d8d59-2cc7-53bf-92ad-6062df331851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21765" :question/problem "A water-treatment holding tank initially holds $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved coagulant.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+8})}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{(\\frac{70}{78})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ab1d837-f782-5439-8e25-3c764f9cb5c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db66dec-2792-5218-8a9d-85b9dbca389e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228334} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4e072-1f4c-5373-a3d6-0795c277ede4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228335} {:answer/id #uuid "fd684dc0-61aa-5e73-9b90-b9380e77f537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228336} {:answer/id #uuid "37b72754-baa1-5d83-b734-0964e3861b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228337} {:answer/id #uuid "39eb6365-d2f3-5e96-a701-c71bfda37768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228338}] :answer-field/id #uuid "579dbc44-8b7c-5a48-b291-b80099c940f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228333}] :question/id #uuid "5708b503-7d42-5d87-80c5-062229fe4d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336439" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $300L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88de6155-45d7-5e41-957f-9f156de48029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.63kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228372} {:answer/id #uuid "95b25896-da43-5f33-a30b-80cc53612828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.18kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228373} {:answer/id #uuid "fcca15f5-034b-5e1e-8479-8e4adbfae343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228374} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0b95a0-d19f-565c-8843-7cf5db62db3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.99kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228375} {:answer/id #uuid "73bd025a-b93e-50f2-9bdf-0861517b1727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228376}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad053d7-10e4-578d-b606-6bda94abf500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228371}] :question/id #uuid "310f29d9-e5f9-5829-9c84-72d1324e1777" :question/math-academy-id "q-336738" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $600L$ of liquid containing $5kg$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10L/min$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3f26d4-7fd0-5dfc-b30a-66eaf31e8b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377251} {:answer/id #uuid "59e1559d-1a25-5f5b-86a0-b44ad480a144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377252} {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} {:answer/id #uuid "3dca446e-f8bb-5a17-9561-bc2230c8306c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.42\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377254} {:answer/id #uuid "9010ad44-b315-5215-8406-035745dd6ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} :answer-field/id #uuid "753683c1-dfc0-5785-8a4b-d335ca58b088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01365019-dd55-56aa-aa06-e538a69722fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-336743" :question/problem "A processing tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved preservative.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative is pumped into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of preservative in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which preservative enters the tank minus the rate at which preservative leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which preservative enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *preservative* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which preservative exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which preservative leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $18\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+18t-12t=(420+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of preservative in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *preservative* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(70+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.9 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =0.9(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =0.9(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.9(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of preservative. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 0.3(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 21+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-11 \\\\ C & =-11(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)-\\frac{11(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.3(70+t)-11{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =0.3(70+15)-11{(\\frac{70}{70+15})}^{2} \\\\  & =25.5-11{(\\frac{70}{85})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈18.04\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} {:answer/id #uuid "d27a819d-3733-5724-9e0a-425c1701164a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377296} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac1a8be-c213-5433-be78-80bb938c8e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377297} {:answer/id #uuid "dba1b189-1202-5081-99fc-577118ac8811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377298} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a95ae-28ad-5afb-9758-ae8c6c582bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c0a0d5-4bf3-54b2-a43d-067aa74d6301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377294}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "456aacfd-49e4-5a1e-8363-4f5725f307ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-339394" :question/problem "A juice-processing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a liquid solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+20t-30t=(400-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(40-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(40-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q) & =(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (40-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(40-t)^{-4}Q & =0.6(40-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3}) & =0.6(40-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(40-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40-t)^{-3} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(40-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)+C(40-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ C(40-0)^{3}+0.3(40-0) & =4 \\\\ C(40)^{3}+12 & =4 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}(40-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 40\\,(\\frac{40-t}{40})-8{(\\frac{40-t}{40})}^{3} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})-8{(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-8,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-8)=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f681e08e-700d-5f11-a546-1b73d4a5d646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377302} {:answer/id #uuid "b28e0e34-3fc5-53eb-9f95-3e5de6d384b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377303} {:answer/id #uuid "4b12db81-cdb5-5c78-aff0-867122be9ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377304} {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} {:answer/id #uuid "87a222e1-6c03-558b-9d4f-e7aa60b98bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c722bf6-15d0-5bfc-a832-c8c844cbaa11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377301}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35a9dbe2-5b3d-52d0-b235-b8068a9e0f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-339396" :question/problem "A fruit syrup tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sweetener.\nA solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sweetener is pumped into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sweetener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sweetener enters the tank minus the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sweetener enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sweetener* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $24\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+24t-32t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sweetener in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sweetener* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(32\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(32\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q=1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-4}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.2 \\\\ (30-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(30-t)^{-5}Q & =1.2(30-t)^{-4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4}) & =1.2(30-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4})\\,dt & =\\int 1.2(30-t)^{-4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-4} & =1.2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30-t)^{-3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)+C(30-t)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of sweetener. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ C(30-0)^{4}+0.4(30-0) & =7 \\\\ C(30)^{4}+12 & =7 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}(30-t)^{4} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})-5{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{4} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})-5{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-5,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-5)=17.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7203da68-a7dc-5fa7-a821-14e51c37d796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377309} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c3b57f-b0fe-5ca4-bc6e-be09e8b780b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.97\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377311} {:answer/id #uuid "57a068be-87ec-53cf-a457-ecd6c727cbfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.92\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377312} {:answer/id #uuid "08fda585-15a3-51ba-b2f7-6c5b1fb1b63f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa11ba31-9d56-5bad-87cb-702df2d10932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6bee6b8-8870-5e26-916b-bf3b0601ff8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339399" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor is pumped into the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the tank minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+16t-12t=(400+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(100+t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(100+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)^{3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (100+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(100+t)^{2}Q & =0.8(100+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3}) & =0.8(100+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(100+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t)^{3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(100+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)+\\frac{C}{(100+t)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ 0.2(100+0)+\\frac{C}{(100+0)^{3}} & =6 \\\\ 20+\\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =-14 \\\\ C & =-14(100)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)-\\frac{14(100)^{3}}{(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =0.2(100+t)-14{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =0.2(100+12)-14{(\\frac{100}{100+12})}^{3} \\\\  & =22.4-14{(\\frac{25}{28})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈12.44\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099acc92-7fac-5696-b31e-f1fb2df34af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.58\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377316} {:answer/id #uuid "b84c947f-ccfb-56fc-b617-b376d1a795a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377317} {:answer/id #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377318} {:answer/id #uuid "d84483d6-8474-5c8e-8b9c-2b641cc22149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.67\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377319} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77d91-31cc-548b-acc6-bdc54133b6cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377318} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d825b7-f752-5a4c-9bbe-c55b7b40186e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377315}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46db6f1f-9edd-53d3-9387-f26ef7512b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-339400" :question/problem "A fermentation vat initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient is pumped into the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $10\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+10t-5t=(500+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the vat, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & =100+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=100+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (100+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.6(100+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)) & =0.6(100+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(100+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t) & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(100+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)+\\frac{C}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ 0.3(100+0)+\\frac{C}{100+0} & =8 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{100} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{100} & =-22 \\\\ C & =-2200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)-\\frac{2200}{100+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.3(100+8)-\\frac{2200}{100+8} \\\\  & =32.4-\\frac{2200}{108} \\\\  & ≈12.03\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228931 :question/id #uuid "bbf602f0-1101-55d5-a6b3-9bfc5731e9ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-21766" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank in a confectionery factory can hold up to $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of syrup solution with $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated syrup with concentration $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+18})}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{(\\frac{12}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c652164a-2e94-5e16-a410-5143037af134" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca0d3689-338f-5cd9-a208-fc0127cb03c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205.4lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228340} {:answer/id #uuid "e37d4573-07c4-5a38-bd34-005f49362735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206.2lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228341} {:answer/id #uuid "ec23d4af-22ce-522e-b625-c0d522a2555e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204.3lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228342} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7b83f2-4693-57fa-adf5-870068e75764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201.0lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228343} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3dd93d-4aa8-5824-8cac-97468d441bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228344}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c3983c04-03ad-5d47-a8d4-7e8d2c785a47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac342bc-5d31-58a3-842a-8a4e79ae42dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377193} {:answer/id #uuid "2980a552-e3d5-5564-ab0e-1bbb2358ce8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377194} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} {:answer/id #uuid "49a8983c-5113-540c-9960-edc50602eed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377196} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1780f1-fe83-536d-9909-81d9b471da59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} :answer-field/id #uuid "33da174c-337a-5186-96b2-62c3b16e65a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377192}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98f92dde-10ee-5a2e-ae0c-afe47e1981a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336451" :question/problem "A de-icing salt reservoir can hold up to $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine with $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A stronger brine with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17fcaa1-cd0a-5b81-a422-98bed86e8456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228346} {:answer/id #uuid "cf230f49-5d88-556c-b1ef-9be0df07fa17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.7lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228347} {:answer/id #uuid "f2079ff6-400e-5858-be77-8608b194d8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.0lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228348} {:answer/id #uuid "37221632-d09d-5e81-8be5-0ac427f473d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.5lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228349} {:answer/id #uuid "719abf85-d7e7-5fed-8da5-1c3347fb8a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.2lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228350}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d74abd8b-f96b-59a5-a0ea-b2c09890154a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228345}] :question/id #uuid "138b92a0-177f-5f9d-993c-5b85e485defc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336499" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $210gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $120gal$ of nutrient solution with $30lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66750a16-700f-5c23-a55a-30496608f331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377200} {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67e761-f48e-5810-bebf-b6e7784e791c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377202} {:answer/id #uuid "0facc8a8-1164-510f-a7f4-a0e94be929e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377203} {:answer/id #uuid "77e0c788-e151-5687-a6d5-ff813c78241e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7f9793-5f2d-5fa5-9828-29145c21e291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2fb266c-0e36-5075-b402-7e3b0f1013d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336506" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $21\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=70+21t-14t=7(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10.5-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =10.5(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =10.5(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 10.5(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =10.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3.5(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-15 \\\\ C & =-15(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)-\\frac{15(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.5(10+t)-15{(\\frac{10}{10+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 70+21{t}_{o}-14{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 70+7{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 7{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3.5(10+20)-15{(\\frac{10}{10+20})}^{2} \\\\  & =105-15{(\\frac{10}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈103.3\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d60df00e-8828-5ca8-b2ed-d8bd599eb07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377207} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa07fe2-d9eb-58e7-adb1-02efd7fc679e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377208} {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf4199-9fdf-5b11-9f3c-4a9a9611b20f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377210} {:answer/id #uuid "e6800f5a-8ca4-5d4d-a314-681c9b7bdc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a930be-e052-5424-bf3a-7194b15d9c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47a16983-dbf1-596b-b57d-9f38439fdd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336513" :question/problem "A flavoring tank at a beverage factory can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of flavoring solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved flavoring. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which flavoring enters the tank minus the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of flavoring enters the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *flavoring* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=75+9t-6t=3(25+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *flavoring* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(25+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|25+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(25+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q) & =(25+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (25+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(25+t)Q & =6(25+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2}) & =6(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(25+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(25+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(25+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(25+t)+\\frac{C}{(25+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of flavoring. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(25+0)+\\frac{C}{(25+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 50+\\frac{C}{(25)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{25}^{2}} & =-25 \\\\ C & =-25(25)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(25+t)-\\frac{25(25)^{2}}{(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(25+t)-25{(\\frac{25}{25+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 75+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 75+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =75 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2(25+25)-25{(\\frac{25}{25+25})}^{2} \\\\  & =100-25{(\\frac{25}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈93.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3931a-6dfb-5fbc-8b0f-dbd997b8f656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377214} {:answer/id #uuid "0562dc6c-24dc-5a4f-a3f1-00a900d6d777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377215} {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} {:answer/id #uuid "976b22bc-fda8-59e2-abbb-17c2352700de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377217} {:answer/id #uuid "a8deab91-7c4b-52d1-bbed-ff4091b43b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "159.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df70905-5278-5516-abae-b8ae4602e0e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377213}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "729a2fd5-523f-5eb2-bf2f-20fedeba14f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336516" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $200\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =12(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =12(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 4(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 40+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-30 \\\\ C & =-30(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(10+t)-\\frac{30(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(10+t)-30{(\\frac{10}{10+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $200\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=200$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =200 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(10+30)-30{(\\frac{10}{10+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =160-30{(\\frac{10}{40})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈158.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635d2341-3a33-5ac9-bc90-9aa868281876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385210} {:answer/id #uuid "923fae5f-f36e-59d7-a2f9-4b1a32154401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385211} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc28125-aa6b-5b7d-9978-9da8bc0bd171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385213} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd0d588-4274-52c3-9c9a-c8c45c106ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} :answer-field/id #uuid "711b7022-c824-5eae-90a8-e7d790b19008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c6933cf-ef37-5bf5-8816-b51b8e56e9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336501" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+15t-10t=5(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =9(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =9(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(20+t)-\\frac{40(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(20+t)-40{(\\frac{20}{20+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 100+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 100+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =16\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(16) & =3(20+16)-40{(\\frac{20}{20+16})}^{2} \\\\  & =108-40{(\\frac{20}{36})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈95.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fff374-e865-54f3-a150-c5efb630780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} {:answer/id #uuid "7fddeb67-7d2e-5a6b-8751-43edc5489a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385218} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6744f-c23c-5448-817b-4573055d4ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "129.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385219} {:answer/id #uuid "e29bac7e-c5b7-5397-996e-06f067a62576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385220} {:answer/id #uuid "01805774-f2d7-5739-bed3-d2bf97e1dfc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fff374-e865-54f3-a150-c5efb630780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7d8e34-4951-5d39-86d9-5779f3f14045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d3bb996-2c5a-57e9-940a-6b48f2e05670" :question/math-academy-id "q-336502" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $160\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+6t-4t=2(50+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(50+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|50+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(50+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(50+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q) & =(50+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (50+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(50+t)Q & =6(50+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2}) & =6(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(50+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(50+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(50+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(50+t)+\\frac{C}{(50+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(50+0)+\\frac{C}{(50+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 100+\\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =-75 \\\\ C & =-75(50)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(50+t)-\\frac{75(50)^{2}}{(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(50+t)-75{(\\frac{50}{50+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $160\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=160$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =160 \\\\ 100+6{t}_{o}-4{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 100+2{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =2(50+30)-75{(\\frac{50}{50+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =160-75{(\\frac{50}{80})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈130.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/a7/a77e5fef143d51d984ce41363465af5f210291f6355b59da4302a944b6a49197.png) find ![](images/8a/8aef212f0b07b6c0fb04e3b3c0e415f3c93d698d48414bc0860a6405db20099b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/ae/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/bd/bd1ef582b76affe848465f42a9c78e897d93f1a2b535b7e880da8ab016561451.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/dc/dc10081b11a8cfba96ed4d16d9ca1c6448af7f6198a5550955c2d123a657d339.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/7e/7efe1d8a24967d9509e73d84e1fb73eea1d